On Sunday, April 22, 2012 10:31:59 AM UTC-4, csfor...@gmail.com wrote:
>  I'm curious: Is the variation of NetHack forks a good thing, or a bad thing? 
> Does it's diversity increase user satisfaction, or just serve to splinter a 
> group of nethack fans?

Personally, I feel that the variation of NetHack forks drains 
what could be the most valuable asset to the NetHack community -- 
a group of talented, capable programmers interested in taking the
game further.  UnNetHack, NitroHack, AceHack, SLASH'EM, SporkHack, 
and their counterparts serve to distract people like Patric Mueller,
ais523, Daniel Thaler, Derek Ray, et al, from forming a team and 
moving the core project -- NetHack -- forward.

(My apologies to every variant and project lead I left out of the
above two lists, by the way; the inclusion or exclusion of any 
person or program is in no way reflective of merit.)

There are only two viable futures for NetHack as a videogame:
The DevTeam can resurrect itself and publish a product, which
seems laughably unlikely at this point, or a group of developers
can usurp the DevTeam title, with or without the last DevTeam's 
blessing.

With the NetHack 4 release on April 1st (two days before some fool 
started this very thread), it seems that the latter path has been 
started down.  The developers of NitroHack and AceHack have teamed
up, and clearly they're incorporating a lot of work from UnNetHack
as well.  That's it; that's the DevTeam of the future -- and we'd
all better get on board, either to support or to influence it, if
we want a voice in the future of NetHack.

As far as all the legality arguments in this thread go, they're 
utterly bootless.  Even if the erstwhile DevTeam wanted to sue 
someone, even if that corpse was still drawing breath, a copyright 
holder needs to establish both that he has suffered damages and 
that he has tried to defend his intellectual property in the past 
before he can win a lawsuit.  It might be technically illegal to 
publish a NetHack 4.0, although I doubt it, but it's certainly not 
actionable.

